```
<?php
// Set variables for sunrise and sunset times in your location (adjust as needed)
$dayrise = "06:30";
$daysunset = "18:00";

// Get current hour from server time zone
$currentHour = date('G');

// Determine if it's daytime or nighttime
if ($currentHour >= $dayrise && $currentHour <= $daysunset) {
  // It's daytime - set color to bright yellow
  echo "<div style='width:50%; height:50%; background-color:yellow;'></div>";
} else {
  // It's nighttime - set color to red and orange swirls
  echo "<div style='width:50%; height:50%; background-image:url(https://www.example.com/sun-swirl.jpg);'></div>";
}
?>
```

To make the page dynamic, you could add JavaScript to change the image URL when it's time for sunset. This would require modifying the above code slightly:
```
<?php
// Set variables for sunrise and sunset times in your location (adjust as needed)
$dayrise = "06:30";
$daysunset = "18:00";

// Get current hour from server time zone
$currentHour = date('G');

// Determine if it's daytime or nighttime
if ($currentHour >= $dayrise && $currentHour <= $daysunset) {
  // It's daytime - set color to bright yellow
  echo "<div id='sun' style='width:50%; height:50%; background-color:yellow;'></div>";
} else {
  // It's nighttime - set color to red and orange swirls and change image URL
  echo "<script>document.getElementById('sun').style.backgroundImage='url(\"https://www.example.com/red-and-orange-swirls.jpg\")';</script>";
}
?>
```

In this modified version, we've added an ID attribute to the `div` element so that we can easily select it with JavaScript. We've also added a `<script>` tag at the end of the PHP block to execute JavaScript code. The code inside the script selects the `sun` element and changes its background image to a different one.
Note that this code assumes that there are images named `red-and-orange-swirls.jpg` and `sun-swirl.jpg` available online. You'll need to replace these file names with the actual URLs of your own images.

Certainly! Below is an HTML code that creates a simple webpage with a button. When you click the button, it will display the background image of your current computer screen.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Display Background Image</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            height: 100vh;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }
        .image-container {
            position: relative;
            width: 250px;
            height: 250px;
            overflow: hidden;
        }
        .image-container img {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0;
            left: 0;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            object-fit: cover;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <button onclick="changeBackground()">Change Background</button>

    <div class="image-container">
        <img src="" alt="Computer Background" id="backgroundImage"/>
    </div>

    <script>
        function changeBackground() {
            const url = window.screen.capture;
            document.getElementById('backgroundImage').src = url;
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How It Works:
1. **HTML Structure**: 
   - A `<button>` element triggers the `changeBackground` function when clicked.
   - Inside the button, there is an `<img>` tag where the background image will be displayed.

2. **CSS Styling**:
   - The `.image-container` div holds the image and ensures it scales correctly within its parent container.
   - The `object-fit: cover;` property ensures that the image covers the entire space without distorting it.

3. **JavaScript Functionality**:
   - The `changeBackground` function captures the screenshot of the entire screen using `window.screen.capture`.
   - This captured data is then used as the source for the `img` element inside the `.image-container`.

4. **Button Click Event**:
   - When the button is clicked, the `changeBackground` function is called, causing the browser to capture the current screen shot and set it as the background image.

### Important Notes:
- Ensure you have permission to access and use screenshots from other devices.
- Some browsers may block or restrict capturing screenshots in certain circumstances.
- Be cautious about privacy implications when displaying someone else’s images without their consent.

